  Status: author draft; independent mathematical and Lean-correspondence
  review is pending. Source revision inspected:
  <verbatim|3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464>.

  <section|Scope and conclusion><label|scope-and-conclusion>

  The mathematical section is <verbatim|manuscript/existence/centered-estimates.tex>.
  It proves, under explicit finite-volume centered-budget, transfer,
  symmetry, seam, and planted-cell hypotheses, (i) a fixed-period local
  modulus on compact cyclic patches, (ii) a common expanding-period modulus
  on compact generic patches, (iii) an expanding compact bound away from full
  collisions, and (iv) a fixed-period compact bound on <verbatim|NoHit>,
  which permits coordinate ties. It does not prove that the RG trajectory
  supplies the centered budget. The separate next obligation is the actual
  production of that budget from the Wilson/RG estimates.

  The quantifiers are kept in source order. Fixed-period estimates allow a
  mesh tail depending on the period. Expanding estimates have an outer period
  tail and then a mesh tail for each period. For a finite list of
  observables, the common budget is the finite maximum at each chosen depth;
  no monotonicity in depth and no uniformity over all labels or test
  functions is asserted.

  <section|Exact source interfaces inspected><label|exact-source-interfaces-inspected>

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|Prose
  item>>|<cell|<strong|Pinned declarations and paths>>|<cell|<strong|Verified
  mathematical content>>>|<row|<cell|Centered
  budget>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.centeredBudget>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.CenteredBook>; <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Center.lean>>|<cell|At
  volume <verbatim|N>, the observable is represented, belongs at every site
  to the interior of the chamber-positive cone for the centered
  depth-<verbatim|m> tiling, and its normalized copied-product root test is
  at most <verbatim|A>.>>|<row|<cell|Budget quantifiers and producer-facing
  projections>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.CenteredBook.each>,
  <verbatim|.expanding>, <verbatim|.Wcmain>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.CenterPort.book>; same path>|<cell|<verbatim|each>
  is fixed-period then mesh eventuality; <verbatim|expanding> is ordered
  outer-period then inner-mesh eventuality. <verbatim|Wcmain> has constants
  uniform over all period indices and therefore can imply either by
  restriction/cofinal pullback; the stronger expanding <verbatim|CellHyp>
  route is also available.>>|<row|<cell|One-budget finite
  family>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.lib>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Difference.permits>;
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Center.lean>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Equicontinuity.lean>>|<cell|For every common
  depth, take the finite max of the possibly depth-dependent bounds and
  intersect the finite set of tails. Reflected and conjugated observable
  variants are a finite list.>>|<row|<cell|Centered transfer
  column>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.Difference.reading>,
  <verbatim|YM.Slice.Time.organized>, <verbatim|YM.Slice.Time.powering>;
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Difference.lean>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Slice/Ordered.lean>, <verbatim|Lean/YM/Slice/Calendar.lean>>|<cell|A
  centered local observable is read as a slab brick. Alternating translated
  original and reflected copies in the <verbatim|2s> centered cells, followed
  by the planted inequality, bounds the replicated column root by
  <verbatim|A>. The brick-tour displacement adjusts two free heat gaps
  oppositely and keeps total tour length fixed.>>|<row|<cell|Continuous-kernel
  to sampled-matrix bridge>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Analysis.Quad.approximation>,
  <verbatim|YM.TimeKernel.learns>, <verbatim|YM.TimeKernel.passage>,
  <verbatim|YM.TimeKernel.totality>; <verbatim|Lean/YM/Analysis/Quadrature.lean>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/TimeKernel/Trace.lean>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/TimeKernel/Unit.lean>>|<cell|The physical transfer is a
  continuous kernel on a compact slice space. The source chooses common
  tagged quadratures, whose weighted sampled matrices have entries
  <verbatim|sqrt(mu(C_u))*K(tag_u,tag_v)*sqrt(mu(C_v))>; cyclic traces are
  tagged sums and converge to cyclic kernel integrals. The section spells out
  the same bridge for arbitrary cyclic-list length <verbatim|r>; physical
  tour length <verbatim|L=qw+sum H_i> is kept separate from collapsed
  trace-list length <verbatim|r=q+sum H_i>. It includes the denominator,
  shifted tour traces and replicated column norms. It does not represent the
  original physical transfer as finite-dimensional.>>|<row|<cell|Finite trace
  interpolation>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Holder.estimation>,
  <verbatim|YM.Holder.finite_lip>; <verbatim|Lean/YM/Complex/Gaps.lean>, with
  spectral helpers in <verbatim|Lean/YM/Complex/Holder.lean> and
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Complex/Columns.lean>>|<cell|For <verbatim|L=2sw>, each
  brick insertion has Schatten exponent <verbatim|2s>, hence norm equal to
  the <verbatim|2s>-replica column quantity. The <verbatim|2s>
  original/reflected slab copies occupy the even-numbered cells among
  <verbatim|4s> centered cells, exactly the disjoint placement used by the
  source centered-column argument. A heat factor with exponent <verbatim|h_i>
  uses Schatten exponent <verbatim|L/Re(h_i)> (operator norm if
  <verbatim|Re(h_i)=0>), and norm <verbatim|Z^(Re(h_i)/L)>. Reciprocal
  exponents sum to <verbatim|q/(2s)+sum Re(h_i)/L=1>. The finite-matrix
  Schatten-Hölder bound, the holomorphic gap transfer, Cauchy's derivative
  estimate and segment integration give <verbatim|l/R> times the tour bound.
  Passing along the common quadrature sequence yields the continuous-kernel
  estimate.>>|<row|<cell|Fixed-period and expanding
  moduli>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.Difference.completed>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.TransferLip>; <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Equicontinuity.lean>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Lipschitz.lean>>|<cell|Fixed compact cyclic
  sets have a compact thickening with positive minimum distance to all
  coordinate period hyperplanes. Compact generic sets retain uniform
  coordinate separation in all sufficiently large periods. Telescope over the
  <verbatim|4q> scalar coordinates through an enlarged compact neighborhood.
  The expanding modulus is uniform in the outer period and has an inner mesh
  threshold per period.>>|<row|<cell|Fixed <verbatim|NoHit> separation and
  expanding collision-free separation>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.Bound.cyclic>,
  <verbatim|.volume>, <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Gaps.allGrows>;
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Integrate.lean>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Gaps.lean>>|<cell|For each pair use the
  maximum over four axes of distance to the period lattice; its compact
  minimum is positive on <verbatim|NoHit>, even if individual coordinates
  tie. For expanding periods and compact <verbatim|Conf>, the maximum
  absolute coordinate difference is bounded below and eventually equals torus
  distance for all coordinate differences. Taking the minimum over axes would
  be wrong.>>|<row|<cell|Coarse/fine cell
  packing>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.Around.image_>,
  <verbatim|.fits_budget>, <verbatim|.figure>, <verbatim|.interior_from>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Around.size_done>,
  <verbatim|YM.Cells.Tiling.planted>; <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Around.lean>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Held.lean>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Cells/Embedded.lean>>|<cell|A coarse depth-<verbatim|m>
  tiling is translated so each point is in its fine-cell margin; a boundary
  count proves placement when <verbatim|6q a_k\<less\>a_m>. Some-coordinate
  separation makes the coarse cells distinct. First <verbatim|Around.swept>
  matches the entire coarse-copy orbit of a home observable injectively to
  fine centered cells and applies the fine planted inequality, giving the
  coarse root bound. For an arbitrary pointed site,
  <verbatim|Around.interior_from> brackets each of four coordinates by
  fine-grid endpoints (16 corners), proves chamber interiority in the
  enlarged margin, and applies the discrete log-convex maximum principle. The
  latter follows by shifting the full replicated product one step, splitting
  copies into the two signed translates, and pairing opposite-parity cells;
  this gives <verbatim|u_j^M \<less\>= u_{j-1}^{M/2}u_{j+1}^{M/2}>. The
  coarse planted inequality then bounds the tuple by <verbatim|A^q>. The
  proof handles <verbatim|q=0,1> separately before taking pair-separation
  minima.>>>>>

  <section|Remaining upstream quantitative
  obligation><label|remaining-upstream-quantitative-obligation>

  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Wcmain> is proved by
  <verbatim|YM.Input.Metered.rootwise> in
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Overlay/Assembly.lean>, itself constructed by
  <verbatim|YM.Extra.Multi.centered> in <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extra/MULTISOURCE_BOUNDS/Application.lean>;
  another producer candidate is <verbatim|YM.MergeProof.Accent.gardena> in
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/MergeProof/Gardens.lean>. Its exact target is a centered
  bound, for every grade <verbatim|d>, radius <verbatim|R\<gtr\>0> and
  exponent slack <verbatim|epsilon\<gtr\>0>, with constants <verbatim|A,m_0>
  independent of period, for all depths <verbatim|m\<gtr\>=C.ref d>,
  eventually in volume index, all period indices, and all labels of grade
  <verbatim|d> and norm at most <verbatim|R>, with root bound <verbatim|A
  a_m^(-d-epsilon)>. The same volume tail works for all period indices, so
  this is directly strong enough to imply the fixed and expanding
  <verbatim|CenteredBook> clauses by restriction to a fixed period or the
  cofinal period schedule.

  The concrete producer has a substantial prerequisite bundle:
  <verbatim|C.Makes> (Wilson selected representation, sound sections,
  grade-stage bounds, stencil placement, price bounds, calibrated transfer),
  plus the assembly hypotheses including <verbatim|Offer>,
  <verbatim|Prepared>, <verbatim|Thick>, gauge simplicity, and the relevant
  multi-source centered estimates. These are not proved in this section. The
  next unit must unpack the actual producer and prove the budget estimate
  from its quantitative upstream assumptions, rather than cite
  <verbatim|Wcmain> as an unexplained theorem.

  <section|Literature and checks><label|literature-and-checks>

  No relevant accepted literature note in <verbatim|blueprint/background/>
  was found that replaces the project-specific transfer, centered-cell, or
  packing estimates. For the general Schatten Hölder inequality in the trace
  interpolation, the manuscript now cites Tropp, <em|Matrix Analysis> (2022),
  Theorem 6.32, and states how its unitarily invariant norm form specializes
  to the two-factor Schatten inequality, including the operator-norm
  endpoint. The section proves the specific exponent matching and
  continuous-kernel quadrature passage needed here.

  Commands actually run:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Read <verbatim|CONTRIBUTING.md>, <verbatim|docs/coordination.md>,
    <verbatim|docs/proof-standard.md>, <verbatim|docs/notation.md>,
    <verbatim|blueprint/background/README.md>, and
    <verbatim|templates/proof-unit/README.md>.

    <item>Inspected the declarations and immediate dependencies listed above
    with targeted <verbatim|rg> and <verbatim|sed> at the pinned revision. No
    Lean source was changed.

    <item><verbatim|python3 scripts/check_blueprint.py> was attempted earlier
    and stopped because this checkout has no
    <verbatim|blueprint/units/YM-000026/unit.json>. The task owns only this
    evidence file and the TeX section, so no metadata file was added.

    <item>A temporary LaTeX wrapper compiled the current section successfully
    with <verbatim|latexmk -pdf -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error>; it
    produced a seven-page PDF. Rendered all seven pages with
    <verbatim|pdftoppm> and visually inspected them.

    <item>No Lean build has been run. Independent mathematical and
    Lean-correspondence reviews are pending.
  </itemize-minus>

